<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrievs Niedra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrievs Niedra was a Latvian Lutheran pastor and writer who headed a pro-German puppet government installed after the German coup at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> in April 1919, in opposition to the national government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>. His government collapsed after the defeat of the German forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cesis">Battle of Cēsis</a> in June 1919. He was later convicted of treason and expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrievs-niedra.jpg" alt="Andrievs Niedra" width="290" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NiedraAndrievs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrievs Niedra, whose name was written Andreews Needra in the old Latvian orthography, was born on 8 February 1871. He began to publish young: his first collection of poems appeared when he was nineteen, and while still in his teens he was writing stories drawn from history and folklore for the newspaper Baltijas Vēstnesis. From 1890 to 1899 he studied theology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Dorpat</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a>, and he became a Lutheran pastor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His stories, criticism and plays blended realistic fantasy with idealism. They often dealt with the formation of a Latvian intelligentsia and with the position of the peasants under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic Germans</a> who dominated the land. Niedra held that a society could develop only through gradual evolution and not through revolution. He was a fierce opponent of socialism, and in an increasingly revolutionary society he came to be seen as a reactionary. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-war-of-independence">Latvian War of Independence</a> he headed the German puppet government as prime minister between April and June 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat of the German military authorities with whom he had collaborated, Niedra fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. He came back in 1924, was tried for treason and banished. In exile he became pastor of a German congregation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, took German citizenship and wrote a long work, Tautas nodevēja atmiņas (The Memoirs of a Traitor to the Nation). After the coup d'état of 15 May 1934 the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a> had the first edition of its first part destroyed, and Niedra's works were banned. He returned to Latvia during its occupation by Nazi Germany and died in Riga on 25 September 1942.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrievs Niedra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1919 German forces staged a coup there and installed a puppet cabinet under <b>Andrievs Niedra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tirza Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.14,26.43&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Tirza Parish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Andrievs Niedra"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4834 5240h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4834 5240h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.14,26.43&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
